WEBVTT

00:00:00.688 --> 00:00:06.325
We use physical trajectories to ground the latent representations of a visual world model.

00:00:06.850 --> 00:00:11.100
At test time, it plans from camera images, with no physical state required:

00:00:11.562 --> 00:00:15.337
stacking, inserting, and tossing objects into a goal pose.

00:00:16.023 --> 00:00:17.497
We call it JEPA-x.

00:00:19.892 --> 00:00:22.317
Pixels alone can be ambiguous.

00:00:22.467 --> 00:00:26.592
From the model's camera, these two scenes look almost identical.

00:00:27.285 --> 00:00:28.290
Turn the view:

00:00:28.310 --> 00:00:30.872
in one, the block is held in the jaws.

00:00:30.985 --> 00:00:33.597
In the other, it rests just behind them.

00:00:34.197 --> 00:00:37.335
Under the same lift action, one block comes along.

00:00:37.460 --> 00:00:38.872
The other stays put.

00:00:39.800 --> 00:00:44.687
Before the lift, the pixels barely differ, but the physical state already does.

00:00:44.800 --> 00:00:47.887
So we use physical trajectories to guide training.

00:00:49.487 --> 00:00:52.562
During training, each trajectory comes in two views:

00:00:52.675 --> 00:00:56.612
camera images, and the physical state of the objects and the gripper.

00:00:57.290 --> 00:00:58.627
Both are encoded,

00:00:58.652 --> 00:01:02.765
and one shared predictor rolls either view forward under the same action.

00:01:03.407 --> 00:01:05.995
Each prediction must match both futures:

00:01:06.045 --> 00:01:08.308
the visual one, and the physical one.

00:01:09.030 --> 00:01:13.368
This ties the visual latent to how the physical world actually evolves.

00:01:14.378 --> 00:01:17.453
After training, the physical branch is removed.

00:01:17.603 --> 00:01:20.228
The deployed model needs no physical state,

00:01:20.278 --> 00:01:23.203
and keeps the baseline's architecture and planner.

00:01:24.298 --> 00:01:29.510
To act, the planner samples action sequences, imagines their outcomes with the predictor,

00:01:29.585 --> 00:01:33.135
and executes the one that ends closest to the goal image.

00:01:35.537 --> 00:01:40.225
We compare JEPA-x with a visual-only baseline on the same stacking actions.

00:01:40.777 --> 00:01:43.752
JEPA-x correctly predicts the physical contact:

00:01:44.252 --> 00:01:48.915
after release, the eraser stays on the block, just like the real outcome.

00:01:49.465 --> 00:01:52.927
The visual-only baseline lets it sink into the block.

00:01:54.717 --> 00:01:57.897
In closed-loop control, both models start from the same state,

00:01:57.917 --> 00:02:00.505
with the same goal, planner, and search budget.

00:02:01.117 --> 00:02:04.630
The visual-only baseline drops the eraser onto the table,

00:02:04.717 --> 00:02:08.080
and leaves the block 41 cm from its target.

00:02:08.675 --> 00:02:13.425
JEPA-x stacks the eraser on the block, and places the block on target.

00:02:15.490 --> 00:02:23.065
Across six manipulation families, mean control success rises from 53.6% to 78.2%.

00:02:23.965 --> 00:02:26.890
One model, one planner, one search budget.

00:02:27.002 --> 00:02:29.265
And every task family improves.

00:02:34.155 --> 00:02:35.192
JEPA-x.

00:02:35.630 --> 00:02:39.042
Physical grounding during training, pixels at deployment.
